1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,397
It's a Saturday morning in the summer

2
00:00:02,397 --> 00:00:04,017
of nineteen seventy-four.

3
00:00:04,197 --> 00:00:06,437
The screen door bangs behind you.

4
00:00:06,617 --> 00:00:08,771
Somewhere down the street a lawn mower

5
00:00:08,771 --> 00:00:09,961
starts, then another.

6
00:00:10,141 --> 00:00:12,312
The radio in the kitchen is playing

7
00:00:12,312 --> 00:00:14,298
a song you will still know every

8
00:00:14,298 --> 00:00:16,097
word of fifty years from now.

9
00:00:16,277 --> 00:00:17,637
And the whole town,

10
00:00:17,637 --> 00:00:20,213
it seems, has nowhere special to be.

11
00:00:20,562 --> 00:00:22,884
This is the decade people forget.

12
00:00:23,064 --> 00:00:25,203
The fifties get the diners and the

13
00:00:25,203 --> 00:00:26,083
poodle skirts.

14
00:00:26,263 --> 00:00:28,340
The sixties get the music and the

15
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:28,655
moon.

16
00:00:28,835 --> 00:00:31,339
But the seventies were the years most

17
00:00:31,339 --> 00:00:33,099
of us actually grew up in,

18
00:00:33,099 --> 00:00:34,859
or raised our children in,

19
00:00:34,859 --> 00:00:36,958
or went to work in every single

20
00:00:36,958 --> 00:00:37,229
day.

21
00:00:37,409 --> 00:00:41,519
And small-town America in nineteen seventy-four looked

22
00:00:41,519 --> 00:00:42,890
like nowhere else,

23
00:00:42,890 --> 00:00:44,108
before or since.

24
00:00:44,458 --> 00:00:46,559
So let's take a walk.

25
00:00:46,739 --> 00:00:48,127
Down Main Street,

26
00:00:48,127 --> 00:00:51,476
past the gas lines and the five-and-dime.

27
00:00:51,656 --> 00:00:53,713
Into the living rooms with the wood

28
00:00:53,713 --> 00:00:55,417
paneling and the shag carpet.

29
00:00:55,597 --> 00:00:57,231
Out to the ball field on a

30
00:00:57,231 --> 00:00:58,047
Friday night.

31
00:00:58,227 --> 00:01:02,184
This is small-town America in nineteen seventy-four,

32
00:01:02,184 --> 00:01:03,858
the way it really was.

33
00:01:04,208 --> 00:01:06,270
In nineteen seventy-four,

34
00:01:06,270 --> 00:01:08,992
most small towns still had a real

35
00:01:08,992 --> 00:01:09,734
downtown.

36
00:01:09,914 --> 00:01:12,213
A hardware store where the owner knew

37
00:01:12,213 --> 00:01:14,140
what you needed before you did.

38
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:16,909
A drugstore with a lunch counter.

39
00:01:17,089 --> 00:01:18,881
A bank with a clock out front

40
00:01:18,881 --> 00:01:21,292
that told the time and the temperature.

41
00:01:21,472 --> 00:01:23,372
The shopping mall was coming,

42
00:01:23,372 --> 00:01:25,010
but in a lot of places it

43
00:01:25,010 --> 00:01:26,255
hadn't arrived yet.

44
00:01:26,605 --> 00:01:28,987
The cars parked along the curb were

45
00:01:28,987 --> 00:01:29,600
enormous.

46
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:33,071
Station wagons with wood paneling down the

47
00:01:33,071 --> 00:01:33,542
sides.

48
00:01:33,722 --> 00:01:36,537
Big sedans in harvest gold and avocado

49
00:01:36,537 --> 00:01:39,353
green, with vinyl tops and bench seats

50
00:01:39,353 --> 00:01:41,872
you could fit three people across.

51
00:01:42,052 --> 00:01:44,161
A full tank cost more than it

52
00:01:44,161 --> 00:01:47,070
used to, and everybody was talking about

53
00:01:47,070 --> 00:01:47,288
it.

54
00:01:47,638 --> 00:01:50,083
Because that winter had brought the gas

55
00:01:50,083 --> 00:01:50,459
lines.

56
00:01:50,639 --> 00:01:53,434
After the oil embargo began in October

57
00:01:53,434 --> 00:01:55,346
of nineteen seventy-three,

58
00:01:55,346 --> 00:01:57,479
the price of gasoline jumped,

59
00:01:57,479 --> 00:01:59,759
and in many towns you waited in

60
00:01:59,759 --> 00:02:02,407
a line of cars that stretched around

61
00:02:02,407 --> 00:02:03,143
the block.

62
00:02:03,323 --> 00:02:05,767
Some states let you buy gas only

63
00:02:05,767 --> 00:02:07,294
on odd or even days,

64
00:02:07,294 --> 00:02:10,044
depending on the last number of your

65
00:02:10,044 --> 00:02:11,113
license plate.

66
00:02:11,463 --> 00:02:13,947
Stations hung flags out front.

67
00:02:14,127 --> 00:02:16,472
A green flag meant they had gas.

68
00:02:16,652 --> 00:02:19,450
A yellow flag meant it was rationed.

69
00:02:19,630 --> 00:02:21,999
A red flag meant they were out.

70
00:02:22,179 --> 00:02:24,502
You learned to read those flags from

71
00:02:24,502 --> 00:02:25,534
two blocks away.

72
00:02:25,714 --> 00:02:27,099
And for the first time,

73
00:02:27,099 --> 00:02:29,507
a lot of families started asking whether

74
00:02:29,507 --> 00:02:31,374
the next car should be a little

75
00:02:31,374 --> 00:02:31,856
smaller.

76
00:02:32,206 --> 00:02:34,885
The speed limit on the highway dropped

77
00:02:34,885 --> 00:02:36,859
to fifty-five miles an hour,

78
00:02:36,859 --> 00:02:37,776
to save fuel,

79
00:02:37,776 --> 00:02:39,891
and it stayed there for years.

80
00:02:40,071 --> 00:02:42,720
Truckers talked to each other on CB

81
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:45,217
radios to warn about speed traps,

82
00:02:45,217 --> 00:02:48,167
and before long half the country seemed

83
00:02:48,167 --> 00:02:49,983
to have a CB in the car,

84
00:02:49,983 --> 00:02:52,480
with a handle like Rubber Duck or

85
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:53,237
Night Owl.

86
00:02:53,587 --> 00:02:55,527
On the corner there was still a

87
00:02:55,527 --> 00:02:57,093
full-service gas station.

88
00:02:57,273 --> 00:02:59,467
A bell rang when you drove over

89
00:02:59,467 --> 00:03:01,449
the hose, and a young man in

90
00:03:01,449 --> 00:03:03,643
a uniform came out to pump your

91
00:03:03,643 --> 00:03:05,484
gas, wash your windshield,

92
00:03:05,484 --> 00:03:08,244
and check your oil without being asked.

93
00:03:08,424 --> 00:03:10,215
If you bought enough gallons,

94
00:03:10,215 --> 00:03:12,128
you might even get a free glass

95
00:03:12,128 --> 00:03:13,672
or a set of steak knives.

96
00:03:14,022 --> 00:03:16,883
Step inside a typical house in nineteen

97
00:03:16,883 --> 00:03:20,185
seventy-four and the first thing you'd notice

98
00:03:20,185 --> 00:03:21,138
is the color.

99
00:03:21,318 --> 00:03:21,911
Orange.

100
00:03:22,091 --> 00:03:22,671
Brown.

101
00:03:22,851 --> 00:03:24,070
Harvest gold.

102
00:03:24,250 --> 00:03:25,701
Avocado green.

103
00:03:25,881 --> 00:03:28,081
The refrigerator matched the stove,

104
00:03:28,081 --> 00:03:30,469
and the stove matched the countertops,

105
00:03:30,469 --> 00:03:32,731
and the whole kitchen looked like an

106
00:03:32,731 --> 00:03:33,799
autumn afternoon.

107
00:03:34,150 --> 00:03:36,625
The living room had wood paneling on

108
00:03:36,625 --> 00:03:38,825
the walls and shag carpet on the

109
00:03:38,825 --> 00:03:41,025
floor, deep enough to lose a toy

110
00:03:41,025 --> 00:03:41,232
in.

111
00:03:41,412 --> 00:03:43,913
Some families owned a special rake just

112
00:03:43,913 --> 00:03:45,324
for fluffing the shag.

113
00:03:45,504 --> 00:03:47,985
There was a console television in a

114
00:03:47,985 --> 00:03:50,254
wooden cabinet, and on top of it

115
00:03:50,254 --> 00:03:51,671
sat the rabbit ears,

116
00:03:51,671 --> 00:03:54,365
which you adjusted with one hand while

117
00:03:54,365 --> 00:03:56,492
somebody on the couch shouted,

118
00:03:56,492 --> 00:03:58,122
that's it, right there,

119
00:03:58,122 --> 00:03:58,902
don't move.

120
00:03:59,252 --> 00:04:00,964
There were three networks,

121
00:04:00,964 --> 00:04:03,664
maybe four channels if you counted public

122
00:04:03,664 --> 00:04:06,561
television, and when the stations signed off

123
00:04:06,561 --> 00:04:07,483
late at night,

124
00:04:07,483 --> 00:04:10,051
the national anthem played and then the

125
00:04:10,051 --> 00:04:11,500
screen turned to snow.

126
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:12,878
If you missed a show,

127
00:04:12,878 --> 00:04:13,677
you missed it.

128
00:04:13,857 --> 00:04:15,377
There was no way to watch it

129
00:04:15,377 --> 00:04:15,703
later.

130
00:04:15,883 --> 00:04:17,959
You simply waited for the reruns in

131
00:04:17,959 --> 00:04:18,611
the summer.

132
00:04:18,962 --> 00:04:21,331
The telephone hung on the kitchen wall,

133
00:04:21,331 --> 00:04:23,396
with a cord long enough to stretch

134
00:04:23,396 --> 00:04:26,009
into the pantry for a private conversation.

135
00:04:26,189 --> 00:04:28,075
There was one line for the whole

136
00:04:28,075 --> 00:04:28,488
family.

137
00:04:28,668 --> 00:04:30,377
If your sister was on it,

138
00:04:30,377 --> 00:04:31,129
you waited.

139
00:04:31,309 --> 00:04:33,208
And if you wanted to call the

140
00:04:33,208 --> 00:04:34,190
next town over,

141
00:04:34,190 --> 00:04:35,695
that was long distance,

142
00:04:35,695 --> 00:04:37,136
and you kept it short,

143
00:04:37,136 --> 00:04:39,427
because your father would see it on

144
00:04:39,427 --> 00:04:40,016
the bill.

145
00:04:40,367 --> 00:04:41,988
Dinner was at six o'clock,

146
00:04:41,988 --> 00:04:43,298
at the kitchen table,

147
00:04:43,298 --> 00:04:45,730
and everybody was expected to be there.

148
00:04:45,910 --> 00:04:46,711
Meatloaf.

149
00:04:46,891 --> 00:04:48,134
Tuna casserole.

150
00:04:48,314 --> 00:04:50,939
Hamburger Helper, which had arrived a few

151
00:04:50,939 --> 00:04:53,436
years earlier and promised a whole meal

152
00:04:53,436 --> 00:04:54,908
from one pound of beef.

153
00:04:55,088 --> 00:04:56,678
And on special nights,

154
00:04:56,678 --> 00:04:59,062
a frozen TV dinner in an aluminum

155
00:04:59,062 --> 00:05:01,880
tray, eaten in front of the television.

156
00:05:02,230 --> 00:05:04,506
In the garage there was a second

157
00:05:04,506 --> 00:05:07,849
refrigerator, a workbench with coffee cans full

158
00:05:07,849 --> 00:05:09,271
of nails and screws,

159
00:05:09,271 --> 00:05:11,761
and a lawn mower your father worked

160
00:05:11,761 --> 00:05:12,899
on every spring.

161
00:05:13,079 --> 00:05:15,668
The garage door didn't open by itself.

162
00:05:15,848 --> 00:05:17,302
You got out of the car,

163
00:05:17,302 --> 00:05:18,440
lifted the handle,

164
00:05:18,440 --> 00:05:20,021
and pushed it up by hand,

165
00:05:20,021 --> 00:05:21,096
even in the rain.

166
00:05:21,446 --> 00:05:24,021
For a kid in nineteen seventy-four,

167
00:05:24,021 --> 00:05:25,567
summer meant freedom.

168
00:05:25,747 --> 00:05:28,259
You left after breakfast on a bicycle

169
00:05:28,259 --> 00:05:30,907
with a banana seat and high handlebars,

170
00:05:30,907 --> 00:05:33,215
and you didn't come back until the

171
00:05:33,215 --> 00:05:34,640
streetlights came on.

172
00:05:34,820 --> 00:05:37,398
Nobody had any idea where you were.

173
00:05:37,578 --> 00:05:40,387
Nobody seemed to worry about it either.

174
00:05:40,738 --> 00:05:42,584
You drank from the garden hose.

175
00:05:42,764 --> 00:05:45,200
You played kickball in the street and

176
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:46,977
moved out of the way when a

177
00:05:46,977 --> 00:05:47,570
car came.

178
00:05:47,750 --> 00:05:49,335
You rode in the back of a

179
00:05:49,335 --> 00:05:50,920
pickup truck to the lake,

180
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:52,632
with the wind in your hair,

181
00:05:52,632 --> 00:05:54,217
and nobody thought twice.

182
00:05:54,397 --> 00:05:56,584
In the back seat of the station

183
00:05:56,584 --> 00:05:59,617
wagon, seat belts were stuffed down between

184
00:05:59,617 --> 00:06:01,804
the cushions, mostly forgotten.

185
00:06:02,154 --> 00:06:04,839
On Saturday mornings there were cartoons,

186
00:06:04,839 --> 00:06:07,262
and nothing else mattered until noon.

187
00:06:07,442 --> 00:06:09,732
In the afternoon there might be a

188
00:06:09,732 --> 00:06:12,230
matinee at the movie house downtown,

189
00:06:12,230 --> 00:06:14,104
where a ticket and a box of

190
00:06:14,104 --> 00:06:16,325
candy cost less than a dollar or

191
00:06:16,325 --> 00:06:16,602
two.

192
00:06:16,782 --> 00:06:19,583
And in the summer of nineteen seventy-five,

193
00:06:19,583 --> 00:06:21,406
a movie about a shark kept a

194
00:06:21,406 --> 00:06:23,620
whole generation out of the water.

195
00:06:23,971 --> 00:06:26,148
School meant a long ride on a

196
00:06:26,148 --> 00:06:28,625
yellow bus, a lunch box with your

197
00:06:28,625 --> 00:06:31,403
favorite television show on the side,

198
00:06:31,403 --> 00:06:34,030
and a thermos that never quite kept

199
00:06:34,030 --> 00:06:35,081
the soup warm.

200
00:06:35,261 --> 00:06:37,409
The teacher wrote on a chalkboard.

201
00:06:37,589 --> 00:06:40,809
Tests were printed on a mimeograph machine,

202
00:06:40,809 --> 00:06:43,279
and the pages came out purple and

203
00:06:43,279 --> 00:06:44,927
damp, smelling of ink.

204
00:06:45,107 --> 00:06:48,090
Every kid in America remembers that smell.

205
00:06:48,441 --> 00:06:51,855
Teenagers cruised Main Street on Friday and

206
00:06:51,855 --> 00:06:54,792
Saturday nights, up one side and down

207
00:06:54,792 --> 00:06:56,936
the other, again and again,

208
00:06:56,936 --> 00:06:58,286
radios turned up.

209
00:06:58,466 --> 00:07:00,470
The drive-in at the edge of town

210
00:07:00,470 --> 00:07:02,286
was still open in the summer.

211
00:07:02,466 --> 00:07:04,196
And the local pizza place,

212
00:07:04,196 --> 00:07:06,857
with its red checked tablecloths and its

213
00:07:06,857 --> 00:07:09,319
jukebox, was where you went after the

214
00:07:09,319 --> 00:07:09,652
game.

215
00:07:10,002 --> 00:07:11,220
For the grown-ups,

216
00:07:11,220 --> 00:07:13,996
nineteen seventy-four was a hard year for

217
00:07:13,996 --> 00:07:14,402
money.

218
00:07:14,582 --> 00:07:17,734
Prices were rising faster than anybody could

219
00:07:17,734 --> 00:07:18,378
remember.

220
00:07:18,558 --> 00:07:20,689
The newspapers called it inflation,

221
00:07:20,689 --> 00:07:22,273
and by the end of the year

222
00:07:22,273 --> 00:07:24,282
it was running above ten percent.

223
00:07:24,462 --> 00:07:26,936
A trip to the grocery store cost

224
00:07:26,936 --> 00:07:29,951
noticeably more in December than it had

225
00:07:29,951 --> 00:07:30,801
in January.

226
00:07:31,151 --> 00:07:32,820
In nineteen seventy-four,

227
00:07:32,820 --> 00:07:35,225
the federal minimum wage rose to two

228
00:07:35,225 --> 00:07:36,294
dollars an hour.

229
00:07:36,474 --> 00:07:38,626
A gallon of gas that had cost

230
00:07:38,626 --> 00:07:41,890
around thirty-nine cents the year before now

231
00:07:41,890 --> 00:07:44,636
cost over fifty cents in many places.

232
00:07:44,816 --> 00:07:48,194
A first-class stamp cost ten cents.

233
00:07:48,374 --> 00:07:49,939
People clipped coupons,

234
00:07:49,939 --> 00:07:51,708
planted vegetable gardens,

235
00:07:51,708 --> 00:07:54,293
and turned the thermostat down and put

236
00:07:54,293 --> 00:07:55,178
on a sweater.

237
00:07:55,528 --> 00:07:57,809
Mothers who had stayed home in the

238
00:07:57,809 --> 00:08:00,360
fifties and sixties were going back to

239
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:02,843
work, in banks and stores and offices

240
00:08:02,843 --> 00:08:03,782
and hospitals.

241
00:08:03,962 --> 00:08:05,773
Some needed the second paycheck.

242
00:08:05,953 --> 00:08:07,799
Some simply wanted to.

243
00:08:07,979 --> 00:08:10,881
Either way, the kitchen table conversations were

244
00:08:10,881 --> 00:08:13,419
changing, and so were the families sitting

245
00:08:13,419 --> 00:08:14,144
around them.

246
00:08:14,495 --> 00:08:17,131
Still, a lot of small towns were

247
00:08:17,131 --> 00:08:19,603
built around one big employer.

248
00:08:19,783 --> 00:08:21,310
A mill, a mine,

249
00:08:21,310 --> 00:08:24,160
a plant, or a railroad yard.

250
00:08:24,340 --> 00:08:26,464
The whistle blew at the change of

251
00:08:26,464 --> 00:08:28,653
shift, and half the town seemed to

252
00:08:28,653 --> 00:08:30,133
be on the road at once.

253
00:08:30,313 --> 00:08:32,478
A man with a high school diploma

254
00:08:32,478 --> 00:08:34,845
could still find steady work there,

255
00:08:34,845 --> 00:08:36,942
with a union card and a pension

256
00:08:36,942 --> 00:08:38,092
at the end of it.

257
00:08:38,442 --> 00:08:40,861
And on the farms around those towns,

258
00:08:40,861 --> 00:08:43,414
families still worked the land the way

259
00:08:43,414 --> 00:08:44,624
their parents had,

260
00:08:44,624 --> 00:08:46,371
only with bigger tractors.

261
00:08:46,551 --> 00:08:48,221
The county fair in August was the

262
00:08:48,221 --> 00:08:49,384
high point of the year.

263
00:08:49,564 --> 00:08:52,141
Blue ribbons for the best pie,

264
00:08:52,141 --> 00:08:53,430
the best steer,

265
00:08:53,430 --> 00:08:54,719
the best quilt.

266
00:08:54,899 --> 00:08:57,036
A Ferris wheel lit up against the

267
00:08:57,036 --> 00:08:57,813
dark fields.

268
00:08:58,163 --> 00:08:59,979
On Friday night in the fall,

269
00:08:59,979 --> 00:09:01,990
the whole town went to the high

270
00:09:01,990 --> 00:09:03,352
school football game.

271
00:09:03,532 --> 00:09:05,204
The bleachers were full.

272
00:09:05,384 --> 00:09:07,509
The band played under the lights.

273
00:09:07,689 --> 00:09:10,275
You could smell popcorn and cold air

274
00:09:10,275 --> 00:09:12,071
and somebody's cigarette,

275
00:09:12,071 --> 00:09:15,376
and afterward everybody drove slowly down Main

276
00:09:15,376 --> 00:09:18,106
Street honking their horns if the home

277
00:09:18,106 --> 00:09:18,753
team won.

278
00:09:19,103 --> 00:09:20,794
Sunday morning was church,

279
00:09:20,794 --> 00:09:22,161
in your good clothes,

280
00:09:22,161 --> 00:09:24,178
followed by a big dinner in the

281
00:09:24,178 --> 00:09:25,349
middle of the day.

282
00:09:25,529 --> 00:09:27,212
Most stores were closed.

283
00:09:27,392 --> 00:09:28,588
The streets were quiet.

284
00:09:28,768 --> 00:09:29,941
In the afternoon,

285
